## The idea

Walk the nested list **level by level** (breadth-first). Keep a running sum of every integer seen so far. After each level, add that running sum to the total. An integer found on level 1 is then added once for level 1, again for level 2, and so on — so deeper integers are added fewer times. That is exactly the "inverse depth" weight, and we never need to know the maximum depth in advance.

## Step by step

1. Put the top-level items in a queue.
2. For each level: take out exactly the items of this level. Integers go into the running sum; lists put their children into the queue for the next level.
3. After the level, add the running sum to the total. If this level contained at least one integer, remember the total as the answer so far.
4. Levels that contain only empty lists must not count — that is why we only update the answer when a level had an integer.
5. For the forward version (weight = depth), walk the same way and multiply each integer by its level number.

## Worked example

**Input:** [1, [4, [6]]]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| Level 1 | integer 1 → running sum = 1 → total = 1 |
| Level 2 | integer 4 → running sum = 5 → total = 1 + 5 = 6 |
| Level 3 | integer 6 → running sum = 11 → total = 6 + 11 = 17 |
| Check | 1 was added 3 times, 4 twice, 6 once: 1×3 + 4×2 + 6×1 = 17 |

**Result:** 17

## Cost

O(n) time, where n is the total number of integers and lists. Memory O(width of the widest level) for the queue. No recursion, so very deep nesting is safe.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// NestedInteger is provided (Models.cs): IsInteger(), GetInteger(), GetList().
public static class NestedWeights {

    // weight = maxDepth - depth + 1   (deeper integers count less)
    public static int DepthSumInverse(IList<NestedInteger> nestedList) {
        if (nestedList == null) return 0;

        Queue<NestedInteger> queue = new Queue<NestedInteger>();
        foreach (NestedInteger item in nestedList) queue.Enqueue(item);

        int runningSum = 0;   // sum of every integer seen so far, on any level
        int total = 0;        // runningSum added once per level
        int answer = 0;       // total as of the deepest level that had an integer

        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            int levelSize = queue.Count;      // how many items belong to this level
            bool levelHasInteger = false;

            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
                NestedInteger item = queue.Dequeue();
                if (item.IsInteger()) {
                    runningSum += item.GetInteger();
                    levelHasInteger = true;
                } else {
                    foreach (NestedInteger child in item.GetList()) queue.Enqueue(child);   // next level
                }
            }

            total += runningSum;
            if (levelHasInteger) answer = total;   // a level of only empty lists must not count
        }
        return answer;
    }

    // weight = depth   (top level is 1, deeper integers count more)
    public static int DepthSum(IList<NestedInteger> nestedList) {
        if (nestedList == null) return 0;

        Queue<NestedInteger> queue = new Queue<NestedInteger>();
        foreach (NestedInteger item in nestedList) queue.Enqueue(item);

        int depth = 1;
        int total = 0;
        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            int levelSize = queue.Count;
            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
                NestedInteger item = queue.Dequeue();
                if (item.IsInteger()) total += item.GetInteger() * depth;
                else foreach (NestedInteger child in item.GetList()) queue.Enqueue(child);
            }
            depth++;
        }
        return total;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Empty lists? Negative numbers? Deep nesting?**

Empty lists do not set the depth; negatives fine; iterate, do not recurse

**Q: One pass or two?**

One BFS pass with a running sum

**Q: Shared state?**

None — the input is read-only

**Q: A nested structure from a 1 GB JSON file.**

Stream it with a depth counter; you never need the tree

**Q: How would you test it?**

Compare with a two-pass reference on random nestings

